    <h1 class="rv in" style="font-size:clamp(2.2rem,5vw,4rem);max-width:20ch">Does Madeira Have Tides, and Do They Matter on a Boat Trip?</h1>
    <div class="artmeta rv in d1"><span>30 November 2026</span><span>5 min read</span><span>Funchal · Madeira</span></div>
  </div>
</header>

<section class="pad">
  <div class="wrap">
    <article class="article rv">
      <p class="lede">Madeira has tides, two highs and two lows a day, and in Funchal the difference between them runs from under 1 metre on neap tides to a little over 2 metres on spring tides. On a boat you will hardly notice, but on rocks, pebble beaches and natural pools it can change a lot.</p>

      <h2>How big are the tides in Madeira?</h2><p>Small to moderate. The tide here is semi-diurnal: two highs and two lows in roughly 24 hours and 50 minutes, so each high water comes about 50 minutes later than the day before. The gap between a high and the next low is a little over six hours.</p><p>How far the water rises depends on the moon. Spring tides, the biggest, come around new moon and full moon. Neap tides, the smallest, come around the half moons. The biggest range usually arrives a day or two after the full or new moon: in November 2026 it fell on the 26th, two days after the full moon. This is what that looked like in the tide predictions for Funchal in November 2026:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Day</th><th>High water</th><th>Low water</th><th>Range</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tuesday 17 November 2026, neap tide</td><td>1.87 m at 07:02</td><td>1.00 m at 13:42</td><td>about 0.9 m</td></tr><tr><td>Thursday 26 November 2026, spring tide</td><td>2.53 m at 02:20</td><td>0.37 m at 08:34</td><td>about 2.2 m</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Heights are measured above chart datum, the low reference level used on nautical charts. Older harbour records for Funchal give an average range of about 1.5 metres and a largest observed range of about 2.6 metres. By world standards that is a modest tide, nothing like the swings of more than 10 metres in the Bristol Channel or around Mont-Saint-Michel.</p><h2>Why are the tides here so small?</h2><p>Because Madeira stands in the open ocean. Big tides build up where the tidal wave is squeezed onto a wide, shallow continental shelf or funnelled into a narrowing bay, as in the English Channel. Madeira has neither. It is the top of a volcano rising from water thousands of metres deep, with only a narrow shelf around it, so the tide simply lifts and lowers the sea around the island without piling it up.</p><p>The same is true across the open Atlantic islands. Small tides are one reason the coast here looks much the same at ten in the morning as at four in the afternoon, with no wide sands appearing and disappearing.</p><h2>Do tides matter on a boat trip?</h2><p>Very little. Off Madeira the water is deep close to the shore, so a metre or two of tide does not change where a boat can go. What decides whether a trip runs, and how comfortable it is, is the swell and the wind. Our guide to <a href="/posts/how-to-read-the-sea-forecast-madeira-boat-trip">reading the sea forecast for a boat trip</a> explains what to look at instead.</p><p>That is why Chifbay's departure times are set by the clock and the sun, not the tide. The <a href="/hidden-coves-half-day">Day Trip</a> has two fixed starts, 10:00 and 14:00, while the <a href="/sunset-cruise">Sunset Trip</a> follows the sun and moves through the year. At Marina do Funchal the skipper meets you on the pontoon whatever the tide is doing.</p><h2>Where do tides make a difference?</h2><p>Wherever you get in or out of the sea on foot. A metre of tide hardly matters against a cliff, but on a gently sloping rock shelf it can uncover a wide band of slippery rock.</p><h3>Rocky shores and lidos</h3><p>At low water more weed-covered rock, and more of the rock where sea urchins live, sits at or just below the surface. At sea pools and harbour walls, the last rung of a ladder can end higher above the water. Take more care, and wear water shoes. More on that in <a href="/posts/sea-urchins-when-swimming-in-madeira">how to avoid sea urchins when swimming</a>.</p><h3>Pebble beaches</h3><p>On this coast the beaches are mostly steep stone banks, as at Ribeira Brava, Ponta do Sol and Fajã dos Padres. At high tide with any swell, the waves break on the steepest part, and getting out can mean a scramble over rolling pebbles. At low tide there is usually more beach and a gentler step into the water.</p><h3>Natural pools</h3><p>Pools such as those at Porto Moniz on the north-west tip are filled and refreshed by the sea. At high tide in a big swell, waves can wash over the outer rocks, and pools sometimes close for safety. On a calm day at low tide they are at their quietest. See our guide to <a href="/posts/natural-pools-madeira">Madeira's natural pools</a> for each one.</p><h3>Rock pools and sea caves</h3><p>The small pools in the lava shelves only appear at low water, which makes low tide the time to explore them with children. At sea caves, the tide changes the headroom at the entrance. That matters for kayaks and paddle boards, much less for a boat that stays outside.</p><h3>Jumping from rocks</h3><p>This is where the tide matters most. A rock jump that lands in deep water at high tide can land in shallow water, or on a hidden ledge, six hours later. Never trust a depth you checked on another day or at another time.</p><h2>When should you check the tide?</h2><p>Only when your plan involves rocks. Check it if you want to swim from a rocky shore or a lido ladder, explore rock pools, photograph reflections in a natural pool, or jump from rocks. For anything on a boat, check the swell and the wind first. On a Chifbay trip, leave the rest to the skipper.</p><p>So it depends on what you are doing. If you are only booking a boat trip, you can ignore the tide completely. If you are spending the afternoon after it on the rocks at a lido, look it up. The Instituto Hidrográfico, Portugal's hydrographic office, publishes the official tide tables for Funchal, and most weather and surf apps show the daily highs and lows.</p><h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>What is the tidal range in Funchal?</h3><p>Usually between about 1 and 2 metres. In the November 2026 predictions it ran from about 0.9 metres on neap tides to about 2.2 metres on spring tides.</p><h3>Does the tide affect boat trips in Madeira?</h3><p>Hardly at all. Departure times and safety depend on the swell and the wind, not the tide.</p><h3>When is low tide best in Madeira?</h3><p>For exploring rock pools and for a gentler way in at pebble beaches, as long as you take care on the wet rocks.</p><h3>Where can I find tide times for Madeira?</h3><p>The Instituto Hidrográfico publishes the official tables for Funchal, and most weather and surf apps show the daily highs and lows.</p>

      <blockquote>In Madeira the tide moves the shoreline, not the boat. Watch it when your feet are on the rocks.</blockquote>
    </article>
    <p class="pref-src" data-pref-source><span>Enjoying our Madeira guides?</span> <a href="https://www.google.com/preferences/source?q=chifbay.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="18" height="18" aria-hidden="true"><path fill="#4285F4" d="M23.5 12.3c0-.8-.1-1.6-.2-2.3H12v4.5h6.5a5.6 5.6 0 0 1-2.4 3.6v3h3.9c2.2-2.1 3.5-5.1 3.5-8.8z"/><path fill="#34A853" d="M12 24c3.2 0 6-1.1 7.9-2.9l-3.9-3c-1.1.7-2.4 1.2-4 1.2-3.1 0-5.7-2.1-6.6-4.9h-4v3.1A12 12 0 0 0 12 24z"/><path fill="#FBBC05" d="M5.4 14.4a7.2 7.2 0 0 1 0-4.7V6.6h-4a12 12 0 0 0 0 10.8l4-3z"/><path fill="#EA4335" d="M12 4.8c1.7 0 3.3.6 4.6 1.8l3.4-3.4A12 12 0 0 0 1.4 6.6l4 3.1C6.3 6.9 8.9 4.8 12 4.8z"/></svg>Add Chifbay as a preferred source on Google</a></p>

